23
00:11:32.000 --> 00:12:00.000
The following year, at the government's urging and with its funding, the TVA began construction on its first coal-fired power plant. It meant that at least some of the TVA's power would no longer depend on the weather. After World War II, Tennessee stayed in the bomb-making business. This time, there was a need for uranium enrichment for the Cold War nuclear arsenal. And so, the demand for TVA's electricity kept going up.

24
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:25.000
After that, it was the Cold War. Oak Ridge did not shut down after Hiroshima, right? Just the opposite. Oak Ridge is still in the bomb making game and TVA had to supply power for it. Almost half its power at one point went to the government bomb making facilities in Oak Ridge. Meanwhile, the South was also seeing an uptick in population. AC became more widely available, air conditioning, so it was like more tolerable to live here.

